Dependency Injection in Go is a design pattern where we try to have a loose coupling. We use interfaces to implement dependency injection. With this, the instances instead of directly depending on concrete implementations, they depend on interfaces. When there are multiple implementations of the interface by different structs, if we instantiate with the specific struct then we are fixed to use that particular method only. However when we instantiate with the interface we have the option of using the required implementation of the interface, which is referred to as loose coupling. If we consider an example of making the payment which is the declared function of the interface. Let's say this method's definition is written by two types(structs) Razorpay and Paypal. If we instantiate and use the function of one particular type, then it would become tight coupling which means if we want to shift to the other we need to make the changes manually, however if we instantiate with interface then we have flexibility to choose the required logic with less efforts.
